A chip is one choice to a screen reader.

On Android a filter chip was three nodes: an unnamed, checkable outer node, a
named node inside it, and the label's text with an empty checkbox beside it.
Material draws a filter chip as a checkbox, and every chip here picks one choice
of a row. A chip is said as one control on the node a finger chooses: its label
and hint are its name, its role is a radio button, its selected state is said,
a tap through the accessibility layer chooses it the way a finger does, and
nothing under it is said, Material's checkbox included.

On iOS a chip was already one button with the system's selected trait. The
package's own "Selected" or "Not selected" after it, in English whatever the
phone speaks, said the state twice and is left out.

